 and it was there  where I began thinking that typical  chemical rockets were going to be very  limiting in the future and that we  needed to take a paradigm shift to be  able to truly explore space and 1977 I  became a citizen and I finally applied  to NASA and that day my life changed  completely it was not until May of 1980  that I received a call saying we would  like you to join us in the ninth group  of astronauts the first thing I did is I  called my parents in Costa Rica  my father had began to cry he handed the  phone to my mother who went into orbit  our dr. sparke and science  representative Franklin chang-diaz I  have become the first naturalized  citizen from Latin America who would  become an astronaut  on my first flight we were playing on  our backs  going through the countdown and I  thought to myself I've done this before  I did it when I was a little kid in that  cardboard box and those images came back  to me in that very moment I flew in  space not one but seven times way way  more than I ever imagined while I was  training as an astronaut I really felt  that I had two jobs one was to be an  astronaut but the other one was to work  on solving the transportation problem  when you look at the present-day rockets  it's gonna take you close to seven to  eight months to get to Mars and then  you're gonna have to wait almost two  years until the lining up of the planets  happens again and you can try to jump  back this is not a fast efficient  transportation system and space can be a  harrowing horrible place when things  don't work with a plasma rocket you can  go to Mars in just over a month is  something that allows you to go there  quickly do your exploration and then  come back before Mars and the earth Gale  arranged I don't see how we could get  anywhere in space without the plasma  rocket that we are developing people  think that this is impossible but it's  not  it is difficult it's not impossible our  engine is called the VASIMR engine and  